titre$ COMMON GENERA OF HYPOGEOUS AND GASTERIOD BASIDIOMYCETES OF NORTH AMERICA 
com$adapt GF fev 2018
1,0$ source : http://www.natruffling.org/basidkey.pdf
1,100$ Bibliographie 
1,0$

1,0 $ Sporocarps globose to subglobose, rarely with a stipe; usually hypogeous; gleba either: solid, hard, lacking chambers or composed of a uniformly thickened layer lining a single, large, hollow chamber, usually with an orifice, grooves or openings to the inside or nearly hollow, stuffed with cottony mycelium young but powdery in age or composed of infolded partitions, the partitions either single or multiple and compact, in some nearly becoming solid; texture firm, solid to plasticlike, usually very brittle. Hypogeous members of Subdivision Ascomycotina $ see Trappe, J. M. and M. A. Castellano. 1991. Keys to the genera of truffles (Ascomycetes). McIlvainea 10: 47-65.
1,2 $ Sporocarps globose, subglobose or like an enclosed mushroom, often with a stipe, stipe-columella or sterile base; hypogeous to epigeous; gleba composed of numerous, small or moderately large, distinct chambers or lamellae-like, contorted tramal plates or thin to moderately thick, radiating septae or tramal plates or numerous, tapering, centripetally arranged peridioles or with combinations of chambers and contorted tramal plates; if powdery at maturity then young sporocarps chambered or with radiating tramal plates, the chamber walls thin, breaking down, dehiscent, if appearing solid, then mature chambers filled with moist, gel-like material; texture soft bread-like, firm to tough and rubbery, or cartilaginous, usually not brittle. (Hypogeous and gasteroid members of Subdivision Basidiomycotina)
2,3 $ Sporocarps globose, subglobose to elongated or flattened, occasionally with a thick, matted cluster of basal rhizomorphs but not possessing an obvious external stipe, stipe-columella or well formed, large sterile base; gleba usually not exposed to the surface, but if gleba exposed, then peridium entirely lacking or present in a large patch near the apex; hypogeous, subepigeous or occasionally epigeous; mostly ectomycorrhizal
2,46 $ Sporocarps variously shaped but agaricoid or gasteroid, with a stipe, stipe-columella or sterile base that is evident before sectioning, often resembling a contorted mushroom, an unexpanded mushroom button, or a fully developed but unopened mushroom; with or without a small or large portion of the gleba exposed; hypogeous, subepigeous or frequently epigeous; ectomycorrhizal or saprotrophic
3,4 $ Sporocarps when cut with true vertical section possessing a small or large columella or small sterile base in the gleba or densely matted cluster of rhizomorphs resembling a sterile base
3,31 $ Sporocarps when cut with true vertical section not possessing a columella or sterile base in the gleba
4,5 $ Gleba of centripetally or radially arranged tramal plates or peridioles extending from central columella or sterile base or of chambers evident at youth but becoming powdery at maturity
4,9 $ Gleba composed of numerous, small or large chambers, occasionally requiring a hand lens to be visible
5,0 $ Gleba of radially arranged, tapering black peridioles $ Pyrenogaster
5,6 $ Gleba of radiating tramal plates or chambers in youth but rapidly becoming powdery at maturity
6,7 $ Gleba with persistent central columella
6,8 $ Gleba lacking a columella
7,0 $ Gleba brown, dark brown or black at full development; tramal plates radiating centripetally from spherical  central columella or sterile base $ Radiigera
7,0 $ Gleba black at full development ; tramal plates and chambers pendant from disk of sterile tissue supported by a percurrent columella composed of thick rhizomorphs; peridium thick, leathery, dehiscing $ Sedecula
8,0 $ Thick outer peridium tomentose, and usually splitting into well formed stellate pattern in age; gleba brown to dark brown usually contained in a leathery inner peridium $ Astraeus
8,0 $ Thick outer peridium mostly not splitting into stellate pattern in age, or if so, peridium not tomentose; gleba black, powdery at maturity and not contained in an inner peridium $ Scleroderma
9,10 $ Gleba darkly or brightly colored, usually in the range of brown, pink, yellow, orange, green or blue-green
9,28 $ Gleba typically white or off-white
10,11 $ Gleba gray-brown, brown, red-brown to dark brown
10,19 $ Gleba variously colored, occasionally olive to olive brown but not brown
11,0 $ Sporocarp lacking peridium; reaction to Melzer's reagent giving a dextrinoid reaction $ Protogautieria
11,12 $ Sporocarp with well developed peridium entirely covering gleba; reaction to Melzer's reagent various, but usually not dextrinoid
12,0 $ Gleba of chambers filled with gel-like material $ Destuntzia
12,13 $ Gleba of small or large chambers, containing spores but not filled at maturity with gel-like material
13,14 $ Columella well developed, dendritic or percurrent
13,16 $ Columella poorly developed or lacking; sterile base may be present
14,0 $ Sporocarps usually soft, fleshy, to firm but not cartilaginous or rubbery, columella or stipe-columella present $ Gymnoglossum
14,15 $ Sporocarps usually cartilaginous, tough or rubbery; columella usually diffuse, never a percurrent stipe-columella or columella
15,0 $ Sporocarps medium to large; peridium usually thin, evanescent, white or pale brown; gleba gray brown, red brown, brown or dark brown $ Gautieria
15,0 $ Sporocarps usually small; peridium thick, persistent, white; gleba pink to red brown $ Hysterangium
16,17 $ Gleba strongly amyloid in Melzer's reagent
16,18 $ Gleba not amyloid in Melzer's reagent, but may be slightly dextrinoid
17,0 $ Brown color of gleba usually from discoloring reaction of tramal tissue and not from accumulation of spores; odor strong, oily, of maple syrup or slightly of rubber $ Martellia (including Gymnomyces)
17,0 $ Brown color of gleba usually from accumulation of spores in the glebal chambers; odor absent or different $ Martellia
18,0 $ Fresh sporocarps with strong, penetrating, farinaceous odor $ Hymenogaster
18,0 $ Fresh sporocarps with no or different odor $ Octavianina
19,20 $ Gleba usually some shade of green including yellow green, olive green, blue green
19,22 $ Gleba some shade of pink, yellow, orange, or gray-blue
20,0 $ Columella derived from a stubby sterile base, percurrent or nearly percurrent, dentritic; fleshy but not cartilaginous or gelatinous except in age $ Truncocolumella
20,21 $ Columella absent or simple or branched; glebal tissues cartilaginous or gelatinous
21,0 $ Sporocarps single or loosely clustered, habitat various; cartilaginous and rubbery, the peridium often cleanly separating from the gleba, associated with a variety of tree hosts $ Hysterangium
21,0 $ Sporocarps, tightly clustered, encased in heavy mycelium, hyphal strands, soil and ectomycorrhizal rootlets, moderately gelatinous associated with Eucalyptus $ Chondrogaster
22,23 $ Fresh gleba when cut exuding a latex
22,24 $ Fresh gleba when cut not exuding a latex
23,0 $ Gleba amyloid in Melzer's reagent $ Zelleromyces
23,0 $ Gleba not amyloid in Melzer's reagent; with large orange spores that are visible with a hand-lens embedded in tissue $ Endogone (Zygomycete) 24. Gleba blue-gray at maturityGautieria
24,25 $ Gleba not blue-gray at maturity, but some shade of pink, yellow or  orange
25,0 $ Gleba some shade of pink, associated with Eucalyptus $ Hydnangium
25,26 $ Gleba some shade of yellow or orange
26,27 $ Gleba amyloid in Melzer's reagent
26,0 $ Gleba not amyloid in Melzer's reagent $ Sclerogaster
27,0 $ Columella or sterile base if present poorly developed $ Martellia
27,0 $ Small stipe-columella, columella or sterile base present $ Gymnomyces
28,29 $ Gleba amyloid in Melzer's reagent
28,0 $ Gleba not amyloid in Melzer's reagent $ Octavianina
29,0 $ Fresh sporocarps exuding latex when cut $ Zelleromyces
29,30 $ Fresh sporocarps not exuding latex when cut
30,0 $ Columella or sterile base if present poorly developed $ Martellia
30,0 $ Small stipe-columella, columella or sterile base present $ Gymnomyces
31,32 $ Gleba composed of empty or gel-filled chambers, or powdery at maturity, with or without a latex
31,0 $ Gleba appearing solid or with orange spores embedded in tissue visible with a hand lens, no evidence of chambers surrounded by pale colored veins, with a latex $ Endogone (Zygomycete)
32,33 $ Gleba with chambers filled with thick, colored gel-like material, these delineated by pale colored veins
32,34 $ Gleba composed of chambers that are not filled with a gel-like material at maturity, chambers may appear empty, filled with thin, white latex or with powdery content
33,0 $ Gleba composed of black gel-filled chambers surrounded by pale colored veins; usually with a strong odor of latex paint $ Melanogaster
33,0 $ Gleba yellow, brown, olive, brown or red colored, if black or nearly black then the peridium is colored bright yellow; usually with no or different odor $ Alpova
34,35 $ Gleba white when fresh and mature, and with a scant or profuse white latex
34,36 $ Gleba usually not white at maturity, with or without latex or if white then no latex is present
35,0 $ Gleba with copious latex in fresh specimens; chambers   0.5-3.0 mm broad $ Leucogaster
35,0 $ Gleba with scant latex in fresh specimens, this drying rapidly into a chalky, white surface, often obscuring the chambers; chambers   0.3-0.5 mm broad $ Leucophleps
36,37 $ Gleba strongly amyloid in Melzer's reagent
36,42 $ Gleba not amyloid in Melzer's reagent
37,38 $ Gleba white or off-white at maturity
37,39 $ Gleba more darkly colored at maturity
38,0 $ Sporocarp very light in weight; with a fruity or nauseous odor; and fresh the gleba is dry $ Mycolevis
38,0 $ Sporocarp more dense; with various odors but usually not nauseous, and fresh gleba moist $ Martellia
39,0 $ Fresh sporocarps exuding a copious latex $ Zelleromyces
39,40 $ Fresh sporocarps moist but not exuding a copious latex
40,0 $ Fresh peridium quickly turning vinaceous in KOH $ Rhizopogon
40,41 $ Color of fresh peridium not affected by KOH
41,0 $ Columella or sterile base if present poorly developed; brown color of gleba usually from discoloring of tramal tissue, and not from accumulation of spores $ Martellia
41,0 $ Small stipe-columella, or columella and sterile base present $ Gymnomyces
42,0 $ Gleba at first composed of chambers separated by pale colored veins, but at maturity gleba powdery $ Scleroderma
42,43 $ Gleba not composed of chambers separated by pale colored veins, and is not powdery at maturity
43,0 $ Gleba gray-brown, brown, red-brown to dark brown; usually with a strong, penetrating, farinaceous odor $ Hymenogaster
43,44 $ Gleba variously colored; with a variety of odors but not farinaceous
44,0 $ Gleba some shade of pink (like Entoloma spore print color) at maturity, found only in the southeastern and southwestern United States $ Richoniella
44,45 $ Gleba usually some shade of white, yellow, green or brown but if some shade of pink then not found in the southeast or southwest and gleba bruises black, blue or red
45,0 $ Gleba white to cinnamon brown at maturity, discoloring on exposure; peridium white to pink at maturity, also discoloring upon bruising; scant latex may be present $ Octavianina
45,0 $ Gleba various colors, gleba infrequently discoloring on exposure; peridium various colors, frequently discoloring on bruising, latex usually lacking but if present then from the southeastern United States with sterile veins present in gleba $ Rhizopogon
46,47 $ Gleba chocolate brown, dark purple brown, smoky gray, or black at full development due to coloration of the spores in mass
46,55 $ Gleba white, yellow, orange, green, vinaceous (pale purple brown) or olive yellow, yellow brown, red brown to rusty brown at full development due to coloration of the spores in mass
47,48 $ Stipe or stipe columella tough, woody in mature specimens; found in deserts and arid grasslands and steppe
47,52 $ Stipe or stipe columella fleshy, even in age; found in forests in the Rockies westward and infrequently in deserts and steppe
48,49 $ Peridium dehiscing cleanly around the circumference of margin; gleba black, lamellate, hanging free from disk of pileus
48,50 $ Peridium not dehiscing cleanly, rather, breaking up and weathering randomly; gleba usually powdery at maturity
49,0 $ Stipe-columella with a persistent volva and annulus $ Gyrophragmium
49,0 $ Stipe-columella with volva but lacking an annulus $ Montagnea
50,51 $ Resembling a large unopened Coprinus ("inky cap", or "shaggy-mane"); annulus absent or poorly developed
50,0 $ Resembling a large, unopened Agaricus , often bruising red, or yellow when fresh; volva absent, annulus present $ Longula
51,0 $ Sporocarps large, many reaching 25 cm or so; external portion of stipecolumella prominent with a bulbous base; gleba often maturing from the base of the gleba upward $ Podaxis
51,0 $ Sporocarps typically smaller; all parts of gleba maturing simultaneously; external portion of stipe-columella poorly developed, tapering to the base $ Phellorina
52,0 $ Trama of gleba and stipe-columella colored pale orange to pale red, turning bright red in ETOH $ Brauniellula
52,53 $ Trama of gleba and stipe-columella white or very pale, not turning bright red in ETOH
53,54 $ Sporocarp robust, fleshy
53,0 $ Sporocarp slender , fragile; not necessarily associated with conifers; pileus typically acutely conical; often found on dung or other decomposing organic matter; resembling an enclosed Stropharia or Psilocybe $ Weraroa
54,0 $ Hypogeous to subepigeous associated with Pinaceae; resembling an enclosed or partially enclosed Gomphidius ; gleba sublamellate and not powdery at maturity; usually yellowish near the base of the stipecolumella $ Gomphogaster
54,0 $ Hypogeous to subepigeous associated with Pinaceae in the Pacific Northwest; resembling a large unopened Agaricus button; gleba powdery at maturity; usually bruising vinaceous near the base of the stipecolumella $ Endoptychum
55,56 $ Spores in mass in the chambers or tramal plates white, pale yellow or orange at full development
55,59 $ Spores in mass in the chambers or tramal plates green, olive yellow, olive brown, vinaceous, yellow brown, red brown to rusty brown
56,0 $ Color of glebal trama pink to pale red; sterile base and/or small, nonpercurrent columella present, easily lost or broken; gleba not amyloid in Melzer's reagent; found only in association with Eucalyptus $ Hydnangium
56,57 $ Glebal and tramal characters not as above; may or may not be found with Eucalyptus
57,0 $ Fresh gleba and/or peridium exuding latex when cut; resembling contorted or unopened Lactarius $ Arcangeliella
57,58 $ Neither fresh gleba or peridium exuding latex when cut
58,0 $ Percurrent stipe-columella or columella present; gleba loculate, labyrinthiform, or sublamellate, may or may not be exposed; resembling contorted or unopened Russula $ Macowanites (including Elasmomyces)
58,0 $ Sterile base or at most small, columella present; gleba usually loculate or at most labyrinthiform, not exposed $ Gymnomyces
59,60 $ Spores in mass in the chambers or tramal plates some shade of green to olive brown or vinaceous
59,64 $ Spores in mass in the chambers or tramal plates some shade of yellow brown, red brown to rusty brown
60,0 $ Gleba vinaceous at full development from coloration of spores in mass $ Truncocolumella
60,61 $ Gleba some shade of green, olive yellow to olive brown
61,62 $ Stipe-columella present, percurrent, simple unbranched
61,63 $ Columella present, usually branched, if percurrent then highly branched and obviously delimited from the gleba
62,0 $ Gleba tubular, pendant from substantial pileus disk $ Gastroboletus
62,0 $ Gleba loculate, adhering to the stipe-columella, no substantial pileus disk present $ Rhopalogaster
63,0 $ Sporocarp obpyriforme, in median section with prolonged, tapering stalklike columella, gleba found above, in the thick portion of the sporocarp, the columella dividing the gleba into sharply delimited sectors; found in eastern and southeastern North America $ Phallogaster
63,0 $ Sporocarp subglobose to turbinate or slightly elongated, but usually not obpyriforme; columella typically branching from sterile base $ Truncocolumella
64,65 $ Sporocarp slender; pileus conic, parabolic to cylindric, acute to narrowly obtuse
64,67 $ Sporocarp more robust; pileus subglobose, convex or broadly conical
65,66 $ Pileus surface filamentous under the hand-lens
65,0 $ Pileus surface cellular under the hand lens , at least not distinctly filamentous or with velar patches; gleba lamellate; sporocarp soon deliquescent and slimy; found in pastures or grasslands $ Gastrocybe
66,0 $ Sporocarp fleshy, frail; brightly colored or drab; pileus surface often with evanescent, scattered patches or remnants of a partial veil; gleba sublamellate to chambered; often bruising blue near the base; found in arid or humid regions growing on the ground, in woods and forests, grasslands, or more rarely on woody debris $ Weraroa
66,0 $ Sporocarp dry, tough; drab or dull colored (ie. ochre, tan); pileus surface glabrous; gleba lamellate; not bruising blue; found in arid or seasonally dry regions, usually not in forests $ Galeropsis
67,0 $ Odor strong, penetrating, farinaceous; found only in association with Eucalyptus $ Setchelliogaster
67,68 $ Odor not strong or farinaceous; found in a variety of habitats
68,0 $ Saprophytic on twigs or logs in the springtime, often appearing as snowbanks melt, at high elevations or in mountains from the Rocky Mountains westward; reminiscent of an enclosed Pholiota $ Nivatogastrium
68,69 $ Not saprophytic on twigs or logs; found in grassland, steppe or forests
69,0 $ Sporocarps epigeous and nearly sessile; found in grassland or steppe habitats $ Endoptychum
69,70 $ Sporocarps hypogeous, subepigeous, or rarely epigeous; in conifer forests
70,0 $ Sporocarps hypogeous, subglobose; peridium and stipe-columella bruising instantly blue; glebal plates grayish brown to dark brown from spores; sterile base or stipe-columella present, if stipe-columella percurrent then fragile, the external portion easily lost or broken $ Chamonixia
70,71 $ Sporocarps hypogeous, subepigeous or rarely epigeous; neither peridium nor stipe-columella bruising blue; spore-bearing surface red brown or rusty brown from spores; sturdy, percurrent stipe or stipe-columella present
71,0 $ Sporocarps hypogeous with thick, cobwebby to cottony, unbroken or torn partial veil, spores commonly deposited on the inside surface of the veil; spore-bearing surface lamellate $ Cortinarius
71,0 $ Sporocarps hypogeous, semiepigeous or rarely epigeous, with continuous extension of the peridium protecting the gleba, occasionally separating from the stipe-columella to expose the gleba; gleba labyrinthiform or loculate, no spore deposit except in the chambers themselves $ Thaxterogaster




